## The work left, in order

One list. Each entry is a task with its reasoning attached, because the *why*
is the part that cannot be recovered from the code later. The prose sections
above are what is already built and already decided; nothing here repeats them
except where the task needs the context to be legible on its own.

**How to read the order.** Top to bottom. A task sits after everything it
needs and before everything that needs it — several were moved down from where
they were first written because the thing they depend on turned out to be
later work, and where that happened the entry says so. Groups are named by
subject only. There are no group numbers and no codes: a numbered scheme
outlives its usefulness the moment one group closes, and then the list is a
map of its own history rather than of the work.

**The rules that govern landing one of these.** They were learned expensively
and are repeated in `AGENTS.md` for whoever is building rather than planning:

- **A task is not done until a HUMAN CAN USE IT.** Every entry that adds a
  capability carries its surface — a sand panel, a button, a screen. Tests
  prove a mechanism is correct; they do not let the owner try it, and a
  feature reachable only from `cargo test` cannot be human-tested at all.
  Two standing riders: "obvious from the API" is not a surface, and a panel
  showing nothing must say WHICH nothing it means ("none yet" / "not switched
  on" / "cannot reach anyone"), because an empty state otherwise reads as a
  broken feature.
- **A bug found while building is fixed where it BELONGS**, not worked around
  from where it was found. A workaround makes the earlier work look finished
  while leaving the defect for whoever trusts the list.
- **Finish before advancing.** Anything split out or deferred gets written
  down with its reason, because an entry that quietly means "mostly" is what
  this rule exists to prevent.
- **Landing a task DELETES its entry**, and what was learned goes into the
  prose above. A stale entry for work already built is worse than no entry:
  seven of them had accumulated here by 2026-08-16 and were deleted in the
  same pass that produced this list.
- **Expand freely** when finishing something honestly demands work nobody
  listed. This is a plan, not a contract.

### Lince's own documentation, as Records

New 2026-08-16, and the reason this list was flattened in the first place: the
plan you are reading should be editable inside Lince, as the Records of an
institute's actual work, instead of in a file only a text editor can reach.
Once that round trip exists, the same machinery ships Lince's documentation as
DATA rather than as pages — the Instinct sand hands you Records you can
put in your own store, so a tutorial about Karma can populate Karma and let you
watch it run.

**Why it sits here rather than last with the other unscheduled subjects.** It
is new work and not part of any earlier plan, so by the ordering rule it would
go at the end — but its real dependency is the editor group directly above
(the canvas, the record editor), and everything else it needs is already
built. Placing it by dependency is the rule that wins.

**The authority is the Records, and `docs/` is a projection of them.** Three
things could claim to be the source — the store, the files, the sand's shipped
bundle — and exactly one may. File Sync already mirrors Records into a folder
and reads edits back, so the round trip is not new machinery; what is new is a
second format alongside Markdown that can carry the parts of a Record that are
not body text. "Changing the sand" therefore means exporting a new bundle, not
writing into shipped HTML.

**A bundle of Records that travels as one unit is a Trail** (see "Trail: the
DNA of doing something", renamed from Recipe 2026-08-16 by the owner). First
Steps is a Trail, not a new import path: the mechanism is Trail plus tree
sync, both already described. The name collides with the relations sand's
shipped Trail view mode and that is unresolved — see the tree sync entry.

**BUILT 2026-08-16: the `.lingua` format itself** (`engine::lingua_file`,
`lince.file_sync.format`, a picker and a "Files not synced" list in the Organ
sand). `My Record.lingua` holds a delimited block above its body carrying
what the Record IS, what is true of it, what it links to and its level, and
the body below. Four things the build settled:

- **`@@` is the identity concept, `@` an ordinary assertion.** A Record names
  which of its assertions says what it IS. Rendering both the same way makes
  the format silently lossy — a round trip demotes an identity to a tag and
  nobody notices for months.
- **A link is `[[Title|uid]]`, and a link with no uid is REFUSED.** The title
  is for the reader; the uid is what identifies the Record. Titles collide and
  get renamed, so resolving by title would retarget a link the day two Records
  share a name.
- **The block is generated, and a hand edit to it is reported rather than
  applied OR overwritten.** Writing it back means retracting and asserting on
  somebody's behalf from a text file — the next task. Until then neither side
  is touched, because regenerating over what a person typed is the one outcome
  an unattended loop must never produce. Proven by disabling: without the
  check the edit is silently discarded and the file rewritten. A file written
  by hand IS read in full, because at creation there is nothing to clobber —
  and it is validated BEFORE the Record exists, so an unknown Concept refuses
  the whole file instead of leaving a bare Record behind.
- **Several formats at once, in PRECEDENCE order** — corrected 2026-08-16 the
  same day it was written the other way. The first cut said one format per
  folder, on the grounds that two writable files are two answers to "what is
  the body". The owner's answer is better and is what is built: **each format
  owns what it can EXPRESS.** A Markdown file holds only a body, so a Markdown
  edit can only mean a body edit; `.lingua` carries the body plus everything
  Markdown cannot say. The ambiguity only exists when both changed between two
  passes AND disagree about the body, and an ordered list settles exactly that
  case — which is why the setting is a list rather than a set.
- **A Record dies only when ALL of its files are gone.** Falls out of the same
  change and is not a detail: with one format, a missing file meant a deleted
  Record. With two, deleting the `.md` while the `.lingua` stays is one
  projection removed, not a deletion, and the mirror puts it back. Treating any
  single missing file as a deletion would make tidying a folder destroy data.
  Proven by disabling.
- **A refused file contributes NOTHING, its body included.** Found by test: the
  first version took the body from the highest-precedence changed file before
  checking whether its metadata block was applicable, so a refused file still
  pushed its body through. Half of one edit is the shape of change nobody can
  reason about afterwards.

**The `quantity:` line IS written back, and it becomes true in the database.**
Asked for directly by the owner, and the first cut had it wrong: it rendered
the level and refused to read it, on the grounds that a level is a fold of
Ledger Facts rather than a column. That reasoning was right and the conclusion
was not. The level does not need to stop being a fold for a file to set it —
`Action::SetQuantityExact` appends the exact DIFFERENCE between what the file
says and what the fold currently holds, as an ordinary user-caused Fact. You
write 12, it is 12; the Ledger still explains how it got there, and nothing was
assigned. Exact all the way through: the text is parsed straight to a decimal,
so `3.50` never passes through an f64 on its way to a signed Fact. A unit
travels with it when the Concept exists; an unreadable number is reported and
the level is left alone.

That is also what makes a hand edit to the block partly applicable now: the
block is compared with its level lines removed, so touching only the number is
applied, while touching a concept or a link is still reported and applied to
neither side. And switching format never deletes files written in a format you
dropped: they are a person's text, and changing a setting is not consent to
remove it.


**Three things this work needs turned out to already exist**, checked
2026-08-16 before planning any of it, and each one removes a design decision
rather than adding a task:

- **Workflow position is an ordinary Concept, not an enum.**
  `Action::TransitionRecord` — the Kanban move — retracts and asserts ordinary
  unary concept assertions and optionally sets a quantity. There is no state
  column and no fixed column set, so `@wip` in a `.lingua` file is already the
  same thing the board writes. No `state:` line, and no format change.
- **The Kanban sand already matches a column by a quantity, by concepts, or by
  both**, and ships `-2 @wip` as its own worked example with "Done is quantity
  1" as its default. So the owner's ladder — `1` stable, `0` unplanned, `-1`
  todo, `-2` doing — is not a new overload of quantity; it is the convention
  the board already reads. It also sits correctly on what Instinct chapter 2
  teaches, that a negative quantity is a Need and a positive one a
  Contribution: an open task is something missing, a finished one something
  available, and `0` is free because done is `1` rather than `0`.
  **Pair the number with a concept rather than relying on the number alone** —
  `-2 @wip` — because a bare `-2` is indistinguishable from a Record that
  genuinely needs two of something, and the board supports both halves today.
- **Assignment already exists** as `assigned-to` assertions, with an Assignees
  section in the Record sand. Assigning a task to an agent needs no new
  field — only the write-back below, so that editing the line in a file is a
  real assignment instead of a refused edit.

**What the agent orchestration is actually for.** Several agents work in this
codebase at once. A task Record carrying an assignee and `@wip` lets one agent
see that another holds a piece of work and leave it alone, without a lock, a
branch protocol or a conversation. That only works if editing the file IS the
assignment, which is why write-back leads this list. Two rules ride with it:
an agent assigns ITSELF when given work, and never removes or reassigns
somebody else's line. The second is a guard, not a convention — the refusal
path for hand-edited blocks already exists and reassignment away from another
holder belongs in it.

**BUILT 2026-08-16: the metadata block is written back from disk.** The half
deliberately left out when the format was built, and the gate on everything
below — until it landed, an agent could not assign itself anything, because
editing an existing file's block was reported rather than applied. Three
things the build settled:

- **The diff is against WHAT LINCE LAST WROTE, not against the Record as it
  now stands.** That is what makes it a diff of intent: lines a person added
  become asserts, lines they deleted become retracts, and lines they left
  alone are not touched — so a change made in the app between two ticks is not
  silently reverted by a file that never mentioned it.
- **A changed number is a retract AND an assert.** `assertions::assert` is
  idempotent on `(subject, predicate, object)` and returns the existing row
  untouched, so asserting `@chapter 3` over `@chapter 1` would otherwise keep
  the 1 and report success. Found by writing the test that reads the file back
  a tick later rather than the one that checks the tick reported no conflict.
- **It applies in full or not at all**, by the same never-invent check the
  creation path runs, and the refusal names what failed. Proven by disabling:
  without it, a block naming one unknown Concept applies its other lines and
  leaves a Record half-way through somebody's edit. An identity that moves
  is cleared before the retracts and set after the asserts, because an
  identity assertion must be unary and unquantified and must not point at a
  retracted row in between.

**BUILT 2026-08-16: an Agent is an Actor beside a Person.** `Action::CreateAgent`,
and a "What" picker in the Record sand's creation form offering Record or
Agent. The distinction lives in the Concept DAG — `actor` with `person` and
`agent` beneath it — so asking "is this an Actor" is answered by machinery
that already exists, at the cost of no migration and no Rust rename. Four
things it settled:

- **An Agent is a Record of the SAME type as a Person.** It has to be: what an
  Agent is for is holding work, and the assignee picker lists `kind ===
  "person"`. Giving it its own type would have meant teaching every assignee
  surface about a second one. What it IS — `@agent` as its identity — is what
  distinguishes it, which is exactly what identity assertions are for.
- **The operator is resolved BEFORE anything is created.** Proven by
  disabling: with the check moved after the Record exists, naming a non-Person
  refuses AND leaves an unowned Agent behind — the precise thing the field is
  there to prevent.
- **The Organ half needed nothing.** "Which Person, at which Organ" is
  `@operated-by` plus the origin Organ every Record already carries. Adding a
  column for the second half would have duplicated a fact the store already
  holds.
- **Assignee theft is GOOD PRACTICE, not a guard** (owner's call). An agent
  assigns itself what it is given and does not take what another holds. It was
  designed as a refusal in the write-back path and deliberately dropped: the
  cost of the guard is that a legitimate hand-off becomes a two-step dance,
  and the thing it prevents is an agent misbehaving in a repository its owner
  is watching anyway.

**BUILT 2026-08-16: a hand-written file may carry the uid it is going to
become** (`store::records::create_with_uid`, `nucleus::valid_uid`). Found by
trying to write the seed: a link is `[[Title|uid]]` and a link without a uid is
refused, so files that reference each other have to agree on their uids BEFORE
any of them is adopted — otherwise the folder is only valid after a second
pass, and a folder that is never valid on its own cannot be shipped as a
bundle. Three consequences, all of them from tests:

- **Creation inside one tick is two-phase.** Every Record in the batch is made
  first, and only then are the assertions applied, because asserting a link
  needs its object to exist. The validation pass was widened to match: a link
  may point at a uid another file in the SAME batch claims, and still may not
  point at nothing.
- **A malformed uid is refused at the door**, and this is the guard worth
  having — nothing downstream parses a uid, so `uid: not-a-uid` INSERTs
  perfectly happily and shows up months later as a Record that does not sort
  or compare like the others. Proven by disabling.
- **A collision refuses, and the check is honestly only a better message** —
  the primary key already refuses, and the test still passes with the explicit
  check removed. Written down because a future reader would otherwise assume
  it was load-bearing.

**BUILT 2026-08-16: the Instinct chapters ARE Records** — `docs/records/`, 44
`.lingua` files, one idea per file, with the seven chapter Records they hang
off. Adopted by a real tick in `the_shipped_documentation_folder_is_adoptable_as_records`.
Reading order is in the assertions (`@chapter [[Record|uid]] 3` says both which
chapter and where inside it) and never in a filename or a hand-kept index.

How it was done matters more than that it was done, because it will be done
again for the rest of `docs/`:

- **Mechanically first, judgement second.** `tools/instinct/chapters_to_md.js`
  turns the HTML into Markdown deterministically; only the SPLIT into ideas was
  handed to a model. "Without losing a bit" is checkable for a conversion and a
  matter of opinion for a rewrite, and handing raw HTML to a model asking for
  fidelity is unverifiable by construction.
- **Two checks, because they catch different failures.**
  `tools/instinct/check_lossless.js` asks whether the CONTENT survived — every
  sentence in exactly one body, every mermaid block verbatim, per-chapter counts
  matching (35 diagrams, 280 sentences, 0 missing). `tools/instinct/check_lingua.js`
  asks whether the folder is ADOPTABLE — frozen predicate list, no duplicate or
  malformed uids, every link pointing at a uid some file in the folder claims,
  every link title matching its target. A folder can pass the first and be
  refused in full by a tick, which is why the second exists and why there is
  also a test that ticks the real folder.
- **The predicate list is FROZEN and written down** in
  `tools/instinct/CONTRACT.txt`: `@@idea`, `@@chapter`, `@chapter`,
  `@position`, `@see-also`. Every addition is another refusal somebody has to
  clear by hand in the Ontology sand, so the list is short on purpose.
- **Both checks are run by hand at each re-split.** Nothing calls them, and
  they will rot if that is forgotten — said here rather than assumed.

**The Person → Actor rename was proposed and then dropped, same day.** Kept
here because the reasoning is the kind that gets re-litigated. It is 371 Rust
identifiers and 99 SQL lines, and `store::people::NAMESPACE = "lince.person"`
is a STORED string with `lince.person.standing` already shipped — so it is a
data migration, not a rename. It also buys none of what prompted it:
assignment is `@assigned-to [[Claude|uid]]` whatever the type is called, and
`actor_uid` on a Fact already means "the Person who acted". What the owner
actually wanted was the DISTINCTION, and a Concept parent gives that for
nothing: `actor` over `person` and `agent`. Standing, the four login doors and
dormant absorption are all written about people, and an agent is not one —
which is an argument for keeping the word Person precise, not for spending it.

### Scoping: what a contact may see of you, and what you accept back

The largest single piece of work left, and the one most other things wait on.
Inbound acceptance is built and per-record hiding is built; what is missing is
outbound narrowing per FIELD, in the same one predicate language, so that
"what they see of me" is as expressible as "what I take from them". Detail in
§12 Visibility and in "Inbound Protein".

**A contact cannot tell "withheld by their scope" from "empty", and that is
where it stays.** The absence rule is LOCAL — the sand issued the Protein and
knows what it asked for. A peer does not know our scope, so a narrowed column
arrives indistinguishable from one nobody filled in. Telling them would mean
putting our scope on the wire, which discloses the shape of what we are
hiding. A known limitation, written down so it is not rediscovered as a bug —
never a task, because there is nothing anybody is meant to do about it.

**A Protein selecting on MUTABLE state, firing grants and revokes, is NOT
scheduled.** Deferred 2026-08-14 with its reason. The mid-stream half IS built
— hiding filters only subsequent ops, unhiding replays — and what is missing
is the capability underneath: **nothing in the sync path evaluates a Protein
predicate at all.** Row selection for a contact is an explicit hide LIST, not
a query, so there is no predicate for a Record to cross.

Closing it honestly means three things nothing currently asks for: a
per-contact predicate on the feed, evaluating it per Record per op on the
serve path, and a served-set table so a Record CROSSING the predicate can be
told from one that always matched — crossing detection is the whole point, and
without stored state a serve pass cannot know whether a match is new. That
table is legitimate (written at serve time, not on the write path) but it is
real machinery, and the concrete need it would serve — "keep these Records
from this contact" — is already met by the hide list. Not blocked and not hard
for the wrong reasons: speculative. Live references and scheduled rules both
put predicates near the serve path, and either will say what shape this
actually wants.

**FIXED 2026-08-16: `log_local_tx` resolves `replica_root`, and the leak it
left was wider than the entry said.** The transaction-path logger hardcoded
`replica_root = NULL` while its pool twin resolved it and branched the outbox
on it. The entry blamed "a write made through that path"; what it did not say
is WHICH writes, and that turned out to be the interesting part.

Ordinary assertions take the pool path and were always correct, which is why
this went unnoticed for so long. Four verbs own a transaction and therefore
took the broken one: `set_identity`, `refine`, `concepts::delete` — and
`transition_unary`, **which is the kanban move**. So moving a card that lives
inside a conversation stamped its state change as general-feed and queued it
to every `sync_out` contact. They could not read the message bodies, but the
uid, the concept it moved to, and the fact that something was happening in
there all reached people holding no grant. Proven by reverting: the test names
the op that arrives on the feed.

The fix is `replica::root_for_op_tx`, deliberately kept beside its pool twin
rather than shared: the in-memory pool has one connection, so a pool query
inside an open transaction deadlocks — the same reason `log_local_tx` exists
at all. The two must answer identically, or whether a private write stays
private depends on which logger a caller happened to reach for. No surface:
this removes a defect rather than adding a capability, and the conversation
and board views that were leaking are the ones that stop.

### Your public face

Waits on the scoping above and on nothing else. Decided 2026-08-15 while
building the mailbox: the public read-only face is a separate ORGAN you share a
subset with, never a fully-synced Cell of your own — so a break-in there leaks
only the published subset, and the generator that produces the public page runs
on a box that never held the rest. Detail in "The user's own infrastructure".

### Devices you can narrow instead of only revoking

Small, independent of everything around it, and the humane half of a mechanism
that is already built.

### Scheduling across Cells, Karma included

An Organ with several Cells must decide which of them does recurring work, or
every device does it and the outward consequences are duplicated. One lease
mechanism serves all of it — the Karma rules and the three schedulers that are
not rules — and building it twice is the only way to get it wrong. The prose
spec is `Karma.md` §14.1; the tasks are here, because that file is the
specification and this list is the plan. Detail in "Who does the recurring
work when an Organ has several Cells".

Asked and answered 2026-08-15: the pruning schedule and the Organ polling
scheduler do NOT borrow the designation — both are per-Cell work on this
Cell's own disk and own inbox, and designating either would starve the other
Cells — while transfer delivery retries DO, being the only one that reaches
outward.

### Tree sync: taking a Record and everything under it

Copy first and Copy alone; Move is Copy plus a separate manual deletion a
human presses. Detail in "Tree sync". Three parts of it unify with Transfer
rather than duplicating it: the consent handshake, the retry scheduler and the
delivery-status surface.

### Profile and discovery: the three tiers and the Facade

The largest group by count, and the one whose whole risk is people publishing
more than they meant to. Three tiers — a 245-byte announcement, a card of a
few KB that is the only tier that gossips, and a Facade page fetched on demand
— with the security defaults stated as tasks because a default chosen late is
a default chosen wrong. Needs identity (built) and somewhere to run (the
infrastructure work, built). Detail in "The Organ Profile in three tiers".

**Surface, for the group as a whole:** a profile editor per tier that shows
what each tier reveals, and to whom, BEFORE anything is published.

### Relay Cells, gossip, and asking around

Reaching somebody you have no address for, through the people between you.
Everything here is unbuilt, and two constraints that were written while
building the mailbox live here rather than there, because the thing they
constrain is the relay Cell. Detail in "Multi-hop discovery".

### Storage budgets

Moved down from where it was first planned. Snapshot-plus-truncate is most of
the answer and is built — the log is O(live state) for text as it already was
for scalars. What is left is per-area ceilings, and two of the three areas
they would bound (the Facade cache, quarantine eviction) only exist after the
groups above. Building a budget for absent consumers would be guessing.

### Collab and the editor

No dependents — safe to build in parallel with anything from here on. This
group is almost entirely surface, which is part of why.

### Transport cleanup

Removes things rather than adding them, which is why it is late and why it has
no surface of its own. What it must not do is remove something a surface still
shows — the Organ sand's contact rows read `base_url` today. Both wire-visible
parts ride one epoch rather than each spending a hard cut of every peer.

### Resenha, and the scenarios that need it

Resenha is the manager for running several Linces at once and the simulator
that makes distributed behaviour testable. It sits here because the scenarios
below cannot be written until it exists, and because the rule this project
works by is that nothing is ever labelled "blocked on hardware" — work whose
harness is missing becomes a named scenario that says what it must reproduce
and what it must REPORT, not merely that it converged. Three of the five are
already `#[ignore]`d tests in `crates/engine/tests/dst_deferred.rs`. Detail in
the Resenha section at the end of this document.

The scenarios waiting on it — each must report WHICH path delivered, not just
that state converged:

### Other subjects — listed, not scheduled

Not part of the main line of work, and nothing here is waiting for the work
above to finish: these are parked or optional. They are written as tasks so
the list is a complete inventory, with the parking said plainly underneath.

These are LISTED, not scheduled — §13 Proximity is parked outright (2026-08-09,
the owner's call: Lince should not tell people who is physically close) and the
federation adapters are an optional direction, never a default. They are here
so the list is the whole inventory and nobody re-plans them from scratch; if
the parking is ever lifted, the entries are already written.

# Alexandria
Was a library inside a temple, well maintained and kept, in a city with a port, many travelers where asked to hand their books and manuscripts and receive a copy instead. While there is the common imagination that it was burned, the details are a little conflicting. What remains from the story is the idea of a great body of knowledge, that worked because it was cared for, and the fact that it can suddenly catch fire and be lost. Great care can be put into maintaining and expanding knowledge. It will most likely provide itself useful if used for the meeting of our Needs.

In Lince, the Alexandria vibe means sharing Records as knowledge of what things are, how they work, their consequences, and how to implement them.

That in turn means possibly caring for the building of interfaces and components to access knowledge, learn it and help use it while also helping with the management of knowledge: writing it and sharing it.
